//OUT="1 1 1"

// The three shapes where the grouping actually changes the answer: a true left
// operand followed by an && that is false. Under C precedence all three are 1;
// under one shared tier folded left to right all three would be 0.
//
// Most other combinations agree under either reading — `false && false || true`
// is 1 both ways — which is why a precedence bug here is easy to miss.

fn main
    out "{true || false && false} {true || true && false} {false || true || false && false}"
